{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>And Jacob called the name of the place Peniel, for I have seen Elohim face to face.</b> This is the matter of which I spoke, that Samael sought to blind the eyes of his intellect with the dust of evil speech, so that he would deny the existence of the Holy One, blessed be He, and would not see the face of HaShem. And when my soul escaped by the rising of the dawn, then 'I have seen Elohim face to face' (Genesis 32:31) — to grasp His existence, blessed be He, through the shining of the sun at the rising of the dawn, the opposite of the opinion of Samael, who blinds and defiles and profanes the holy as profane. And this is what it says: 'And the sun rose upon him' (Genesis 32:32), that is, to heal him. And afterward it says, 'and he limped upon his thigh' (Genesis 32:32) — these are difficult together. Rather, it means that through the shining of the sun, healing came to him for the blow that Samael sought to strike him — namely, to blind the eyes of his intellect so as to bring him to the denial of Elohim. Therefore it says 'upon him' — for his need, for 'the sun of righteousness and healing in her wings' (Malachi 3:20) shone upon him, and by reason of its shining 'a king in his beauty shall your eyes behold' (Isaiah 33:17). But in any case, the deeds of Samael were effective to make him limp upon his thigh and to shut against him the gates of understanding, so that he would have no entrance to enter into 'the secret of HaShem for those who fear Him,' all the while that his eye is on his wealth and he rushes after the bulmus of the acquisitions of this world."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:31:47.530446645Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}